  "query_preview": "..The low pressure system that helped funnel in\nmoisture over the last few days is currently centered over the NE\ncorner of New Mexico. With this disturbance exiting the region,\nhigh pressure looks to build over the Southwest for the next\nseveral days. This change in the synoptic pattern will allow for\ntemperatures to gradually warm and drier conditions to return.\nExpect daytime highs to climb near normal for this time of year\nand for minimum RH values to steadily drop through the forecast\nperiod. Before we dry out though, current elevated dew points\ncould lead to some overnight/early morning fog across the High\nCountry. If areas do see this first thing in the morning,\neverything should burn off fairly quickly as daytime heating kicks\nin. \n\nAlthough model guidance is in agreement with high pressure\nlingering over much of the Western US through next week, there is\nsome discrepancies on whether or not a short-wave will clip the\neastern part of the state, and create a few isolated\nshowers/storms across the higher terrain. Confidence is very low\nat this time on whether or not we'll see the return of\nprecipitation, so for now, expect mostly dry conditions for the\nforeseeable future.",
